.TH JITTER_RNG 1 2026-09-30 "jitterentropy x.y.z" "User Commands"
.\" groff re-enables hyphenation per paragraph unless HY is 0.
.nr HY 0
.nh
.SH NAME
jitter_rng \- query the jitter_rng kernel module and read random bytes from it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jitter_rng
.RB [ \-d
.IR device ]
.RB [ \-i | \-s | \-t | \-r
.I bytes
.RB [ \-x ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jitter_rng
shows what the
.BR jitter_rng (4)
kernel module runs with and reads random bytes from its character device,
through
.BR jitterentropy-kernel (3).
Every invocation opens the device once and so works on an instance of its
own.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-info
The default. Print the module's version, configuration and interfaces from
.IR /proc/jitterentropy ,
and the identifier, version, oversampling rate, flags, memory size, hash loop
count, health test failures, reinitializations and output counters of the
instance.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-status
Print the JSON status document of the instance.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-random " \fIbytes\fR"
Write
.I bytes
random bytes to standard output.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-hex
With
.BR \-r ,
write them as hexadecimal digits followed by a newline.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-selftest
Run the cryptographic self test on the instance. Needs
.BR CAP_SYS_ADMIN .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device " \fIdevice\fR"
Open
.I device
instead of
.IR /dev/jitterentropy .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when the device cannot be opened, an argument is invalid or
the request fails.
.SH EXAMPLES
A 256 bit key as hexadecimal digits:
.PP
.EX
jitter_rng \-r 32 \-x
.EE
.PP
The health test state of an instance, with
.BR jq (1):
.PP
.EX
jitter_rng \-s | jq .healthFailure
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jitterentropy-kernel (3),
.BR jitter_rng (4)
